<h1>McCaffrey's No. <em>6</em> Ranking & The Numbers Behind It</h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📖 7 min read</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🦘 Vertical Jump &amp; RAS</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏈 NFL Combine Profiles</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Christian McCaffrey climbed 67 spots to No. 6 on the NFL Top 100 Players of 2026, one of the biggest single-year jumps in the countdown's history. Nine years after his combine, the numbers behind that ranking are still worth a second look — and they tell a story about what actually predicts NFL production.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">
<div class="callout" style="background:var(--surface);border-color:var(--accent);">
<div class="callout-icon">⚡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> McCaffrey jumped from No. 73 to <strong>No. 6</strong> on the NFL Top 100 Players of 2026 after totaling <strong>2,126</strong> scrimmage yards and 17 touchdowns in 2025. At the 2017 combine, he ran a <strong>4.48-second 40</strong>, jumped <strong>37.5 inches</strong> vertically, and posted a <strong>Relative Athletic Score of 8.52</strong> out of 10 — good, not elite, for the position. His agility numbers were the real outlier.</div>
</div>
<h2>A 67-Spot Jump, Nine Years After the Combine</h2>
<p>McCaffrey entered the 2026 NFL Top 100 countdown at No. 73 the year before. He exited it at <strong>No. 6</strong>, the highest of any running back and one of only five players from the entire San Francisco 49ers roster to make the list, joining Trent Williams (No. 40), George Kittle (No. 46), Fred Warner (No. 54), and Brock Purdy (No. 85).</p>
<p>The jump followed a 2025 season where McCaffrey started all 17 games, carried the ball 311 times for 1,202 yards, and led all NFL running backs in receiving with 102 catches for 924 yards. His <strong>2,126</strong> total scrimmage yards were the second-most in the league, and it was the third time in his career he cleared 2,000 yards from scrimmage in a season. The performance earned him First-Team All-Pro honors and the NFL's 2025 Comeback Player of the Year award, coming off an injury-shortened 2024.</p>
<p>What makes the ranking interesting from a testing standpoint is that McCaffrey's <strong>2025 rushing efficiency was among the worst of his career</strong> — 3.9 yards per carry, his second-lowest mark in any season he played 10-plus games. His value to the 49ers came almost entirely from volume, receiving work, and durability, not explosiveness. That's a useful reminder that athletic testing captures potential, not the full picture of what makes a player valuable at the NFL level.</p>
<h2>McCaffrey's 2017 Combine Numbers</h2>
<p>McCaffrey measured in at 5'11", 202 pounds for the 2017 NFL Scouting Combine — light for the running back position even at the time. His testing day put those size concerns to rest as an athleticism question, if not as a workhorse-durability one:</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Drill</th>
<th>Result</th>
<th>Context</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="fast"><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>4.48s</td><td>Fastest of the top-4 RB class (Fournette, Cook, Kamara)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical Jump</td><td>37.5"</td><td>2nd-best among RBs at the 2017 combine</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broad Jump</td><td>10'1"</td><td>Solid, not elite, for the position</td></tr>
<tr class="elite"><td>3-Cone Drill</td><td>6.57s</td><td>2nd-fastest by an RB since 2006</td></tr>
<tr><td>20-Yard Shuttle</td><td>4.22s</td><td>Above-average agility mark</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bench Press</td><td>10 reps</td><td>2nd-fewest among RBs that year</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The bench press was the outlier, and it barely mattered. McCaffrey's <strong>Relative Athletic Score sits at 8.52 out of 10</strong> — strong, but not among the true elite testers at the position, since his weight (a 3.12 score) and bench numbers dragged down an otherwise excellent profile. His agility scores were the standout: a 9.97 for the three-cone and 8.49 for the vertical jump.</p>
<p>Curious where your own testing numbers stack up? Try our <a href="/vertical-jump-percentile">vertical jump percentile calculator</a> to see how a 37.5-inch jump compares across age, sport, and level.</p>
