<h2>Good Testing Numbers, Historic Production</h2>
<p>McCaffrey's combine profile was never the story — his tape and receiving production were. That gap between "very good athlete" and "generational receiving back" is exactly why teams weight game film more heavily than testing numbers for players who show unique skill sets on tape. A 4.48 40 and a 37.5-inch vertical would get most running backs drafted; they don't explain 7,589 career rushing yards, 5,390 receiving yards, and 98 total touchdowns across nine NFL seasons.</p>
<p>What his agility numbers <em>did</em> predict accurately: his three-cone and shuttle times, both elite for the position, hinted at the short-area quickness and route-running ability that made him one of the best pass-catching backs of his generation. Straight-line speed and jumping ability are only part of what NFL running back evaluation actually measures.</p>
<h2>How He Compares to Today's Combine Class</h2>
<p>Measured against this year's incoming rookies, McCaffrey's numbers read as solidly above-average rather than elite. Arkansas running back Mike Washington Jr. ran a <strong>4.33</strong> at the 2026 combine — 15 hundredths faster than McCaffrey's 4.48 nine years earlier — and posted a 99th-percentile weight-adjusted 40 time for the position. The overall running back testing bar has risen across the league, even as McCaffrey's on-field production has aged remarkably well relative to his athletic scores.</p>
